Got: undefined`,question:"Fix?",options:["HashMap approach needed — [3,2,4] has no sorted guarantee","Sort first","Use binary search","Use brute force"],correct:0,explanation:"Two-pointer assumes sorted input: on [3,2,4] the pointers never test 2+4. Either sort first (losing the original indices) or use the hash-map one-pass, which needs no order at all. Preconditions are part of the algorithm.",hint:"What did the two-pointer walk assume about the array that [3,2,4] violates?"}],41:[{type:"pair",prompt:"Advanced concepts",pairs:[{id:1,left:"Amortized O(1)",right:"Avg cost over many ops"},{id:2,left:"NP-Complete",right:"No known fast solution"},{id:3,left:"Space-Time Tradeoff",right:"Memory saves time"},{id:4,left:"Greedy",right:"Local optimal choices"}],explanation:"Amortized is why arr.push is called O(1) despite occasional resizes. NP-complete means no known fast exact algorithm — approximation, not surrender. Memoisation is the space-time trade in its purest form."},{type:"trace",prompt:"Greedy: activity selection?",code:[{text:"// Activities: (start, end)"},{text:"// [(1,4),(3,5),(0,6),(5,7),(8,9),(5,9)]"},{text:"// Sort by end time, pick non-overlapping"},{text:"// Pick: (1,4),(5,7),(8,9)",highlight:!0}],options:["3 activities","2 activities","4 activities","6 activities"],correct:0,explanation:"Take (1,4); skip what starts before 4; take (5,7); skip (5,9); take (8,9) — three. Sorting by END time is the load-bearing choice, and this greedy is provably optimal: finishing earliest leaves maximal room.",hint:"After taking (1,4), the next pick must start at 4 or later. Walk the sorted list."},{type:"realworld",prompt:"When does greedy FAIL?",scenario:"⚠️ Greedy doesnt always give optimal.",options:[{code:`// Coin change: coins=[1,3,4], amount=6
