// table: iteration, computes everything up to n`,label:"Direction and stack usage"},{code:"// the memo version is asymptotically faster",label:"The memo is faster"},{code:"// the table version gives different answers",label:"They differ in results"},{code:"// the memo uses less memory, always",label:"Memo saves memory"}],correct:0,explanation:"Both are O(n) time and produce identical answers — the differences are operational. Top-down computes only subproblems actually reached, but deep chains risk StackOverflowError. Bottom-up never touches the stack, uses a flat primitive array instead of a boxing HashMap, and often admits an O(1)-space squeeze. In Java the iteration + int[] combination is usually the pragmatic winner.",hint:"Rule out the claims about speed and answers first. What operational differences remain?"},{type:"trace",prompt:"Extend the table: what is dp[4]?",code:[{text:"// dp[1]=1, dp[2]=2"},{text:"// dp[3]=dp[2]+dp[1]=3"},{text:"// dp[4]=dp[3]+dp[2]=?",highlight:!0}],options:["4","5","6","8"],correct:1,explanation:"3 + 2 = 5. It is Fibonacci wearing a costume — same recurrence, shifted start. Most counting-paths problems reduce to a known recurrence like this; recognising the shape saves deriving it from scratch. 8 is dp[5], one row further: the classic off-by-one.",hint:"The last two rows are on screen. Add them."}],32:[{type:"trace",prompt:"What does power(2, 10) return?",code:[{text:"long power(long b, int e) {"},{text:"    if (e==0) return 1;"},{text:"    if (e%2==0) { long h=power(b,e/2); return h*h; }"},{text:"    return b*power(b,e-1);"},{text:"}"},{text:"// power(2, 10) = ?",highlight:!0}],options:["20","100","512","1024"],correct:3,explanation:"2¹⁰ = 1024 — the algorithm changes the route, not the value. 20 is 2×10, 100 is 10², 512 is 2⁹, one halving misplaced. The long return type is no accident: int silently overflows at 2³¹, and fast power reaches big numbers fast, so the wide type matters.",hint:"The recursion computes 2 to the 10th, just cleverly. What IS 2¹⁰?"},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Assemble the coin-change DP",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {"},{id:"b",text:"    int[] dp = new int[amount+1];"},{id:"c",text:"    Arrays.fill(dp, amount+1);"},{id:"d",text:"    dp[0] = 0;"},{id:"e",text:"    for (int i=1; i<=amount; i++)"},{id:"f",text:"        for (int c : coins)"},{id:"g",text:"            if (c<=i) dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], dp[i-c]+1);"},{id:"h",text:"    return dp[amount]>amount ? -1 : dp[amount];"},{id:"i",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"j",text:"    dp[0] = 1;"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h","i"],explanation:'amount+1 plays the role of infinity — no real answer can use more than `amount` coins, so anything above it means "unreachable", which the last line converts to -1. Integer.MAX_VALUE would overflow to negative on the +1. The fill must precede dp[0]=0, or the zero gets stamped over. The spare line seeds dp[0]=1: every answer one too high, no crash to warn you.',hint:"Why amount+1 rather than Integer.MAX_VALUE as the sentinel? What happens to MAX_VALUE + 1?"},{type:"realworld",prompt:"Fib benefits from DP; merge sort does not. What is the difference?",scenario:"🧠 Both recurse. Only one is worth caching.",options:[{code:`// fib(n-1) and fib(n-2) RESHARE subcalls
// merge sort halves never overlap`,label:"Overlapping subproblems"},{code:"// fib is arithmetic, sorting is not",label:"Arithmetic vs rearranging"},{code:"// fib recurses twice, merge sort once",label:"Number of recursive calls"},{code:"// fib is defined on integers",label:"Integer inputs"}],correct:0,explanation:"Caching pays only when the same subproblem RECURS: fib(5) and fib(4) both need fib(3). Merge sort's halves are disjoint — no subarray is sorted twice, so a cache would only ever miss. That plus optimal substructure is the two-part test for DP. Merge sort also recurses twice, which kills the third option.",hint:"A cache helps only if something is asked twice. Which algorithm ever repeats a subproblem?"}],33:[{type:"pair",prompt:"Match each representation to its Java type",pairs:[{id:1,left:"Adjacency list",right:"List<List<Integer>>"},{id:2,left:"Adjacency matrix",right:"int[][]"},{id:3,left:"Edge list",right:"List<int[]>"},{id:4,left:"List vs matrix space",right:"O(V+E) vs O(V²)"}],explanation:`The list stores only edges that exist; the matrix pre-pays for every POSSIBLE edge to get O(1) "is there an edge?" checks. The edge list is the input format — algorithms almost always convert it to an adjacency list first, because finding a node's neighbours in an edge list is a full scan.`},{type:"fillgap",prompt:"The lists exist — now record the DIRECTED edge e[0]→e[1]",codeParts:[{text:`List<List<Integer>> buildGraph(int n, int[][] edges) {
